Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical API-testing with event-driven, multi-tenant systems and guarantees like at-least-once delivery, idempotency, and DLQ routing.

Key Concepts
- Idempotency per tenant
- End-to-end delivery guarantees
- Failure modes: broker outage, consumer downtime
- Observability: audit logs and DLQs

Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test: ensure duplicate webhook does not double-process
async function testIdempotentWebhook(httpPost, tenantId, event){
  const first = await httpPost(`/tenants/${tenantId}/webhooks`, event)
  const dup = await httpPost(`/tenants/${tenantId}/webhooks`, event)
  assert(first.status === 200 && dup.status === 200)
  // verify idempotent side-effects only occurred once
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate replay tests against a canary environment?
- How would you verify DLQ routing correctness under mixed failure modes?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical testing of dynamic feature flags and edge propagation, race conditions, and rollback paths under real-world latency. It requires concrete test design, tooling, and observability considerations.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant feature flags and canary
- Edge propagation latency and cache invalidation
- Race condition testing during toggles
- Rollback and observability (metrics/logs)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test sketch
test('canary flag propagation across edge', async () => {
  setFlag('TenantA','newContent',true);
  await wait(35000);
  const rA = await httpGet('/content?tenant=TenantA', headers);
  const rB = await httpGet('/content?tenant=TenantB', headers);
  expect(rA.flag).toBe(true);
  expect(rB.flag).toBe(false);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you automate clock manipulation in test env to simulate propagation windows?
- How would you measure edge propagation latency and flag consistency across regions?

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates practical API versioning and deprecation testing strategies, assessing the candidate's ability to design comprehensive test coverage for versioned APIs with concrete expectations and appropriate tooling recommendations.

## Key Concepts

- Version routing via HTTP headers
- Backward compatibility validation between API versions
- Deprecation signaling and migration path implementation
- Negative testing and edge case handling

## Code Example

```javascript
describe('GET /users/{id} versioned endpoints', () => {
  it('defaults to v1 when Version header is missing', async () => {
    const res = await request.get('/users/123')
    expect(res.body).toHaveProperty('username')
    expect(res.body).toHaveProperty('email')
    expect(res.body).not.toHaveProperty('phone')
  })

  it('returns v2 format with include_email=true', async () => {
    const res = await request
      .get('/users/123?include_email=true')
      .set('X-API-Version', 'v2')
    expect(res.body).toEqual({
      username: expect.any(String),
      email: expect.any(String),
      phone: expect.any(String),
      address: expect.any(Object)
    })
  })
})

## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates a candidate's ability to test asynchronous, event-driven systems with idempotency, resilience patterns, and cross-service contracts. It assesses understanding of production-grade reliability and distributed systems testing.

## Key Concepts

- Asynchronous job processing and polling patterns
- Idempotency keys and deduplication strategies
- Dead-letter queues (DLQs) for failed processing
- Retry mechanisms with exponential backoff
- Schema evolution in event-driven architectures (Kafka)
- Contract testing and observability

## Code Example

```java
@Test
void testIdempotency() {
    String idempotencyKey = UUID.randomUUID().toString();

    Response first = given()
        .contentType("application/json")
        .body(jobPayload)
        .header("Idempotency-Key", idempotencyKey)
        .post("/jobs");

    Response second = given()
        .contentType("application/json")
        .body(jobPayload)
        .header("Idempotency-Key", idempotencyKey)
        .post("/jobs");

    assertEquals(first.jsonPath().getString("jobId"), 
                 second.jsonPath().getString("jobId"));
}

@Test
void testRetryWithBackoff() {
    String jobId = submitJob();

    await().atMost(30, TimeUnit.SECONDS)
           .until(() -> getStatus(jobId).equals("DONE"));

    verify(retries, times(3));
    verify(backoff, times(2)); // exponential: 1s, 2s
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Examines ability to test dynamic, central policy-driven routing at scale and under failure modes.

## Key Concepts

- Central policy propagation across distributed nodes
- Race conditions during config reloads
- Per-tenant overrides and canary validation
- Observability, metrics, and alerting for policy health

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test: verify policy reloads propagate within short window
async function testPolicyPropagation(nodePool, policyApi) {
  await policyApi.forceUpdate();
  const results = await Promise.all(nodePool.map(n => n.getCurrentPolicyVersion()));
  return results.every(v => v === results[0]);
}
```

